.\" Man page for isb
.TH ISB 1 "July 2026" "isb" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
isb \- Intelligent Subsystem Backport for RHEL kernels
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isb
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
Must be run from inside a RHEL kernel git repository.
Session parameters are prompted interactively on first run and
reused automatically on subsequent runs.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isb
cherry-picks upstream Linux kernel commits for a given subsystem into a
RHEL or CentOS Stream kernel tree.
It discovers relevant scope paths (source directories, headers,
documentation, device-tree bindings), finds the divergence point
between upstream and RHEL, filters out already-applied commits, and
applies the remaining ones with RH Kernel Work Flow specified commit messages suitable
for RHEL merge requests.
.PP
Out-of-scope files touched by tree-wide commits are automatically
excluded.
In-scope conflicts are resolved according to the selected
.BR \-\-conflict\-policy :
.B patchpal
(launch the
.BR patchpal (1)
GUI for interactive three-way merge),
.B edit
(open conflicted files in the user's editor),
.B ai
(Cursor AI Rule \(em Opus agent resolves automatically), or
.B abort
(stop on conflict).
The default is
.B ai
(Cursor AI Rule)
if cursor\-sdk and CURSOR_API_KEY are available, then
.B patchpal
if a graphical display and
.BR patchpal (1)
are present, otherwise
.BR edit .
.PP
The Cursor AI Rule
.RB ( ai )
policy uses a Cursor Opus agent to resolve conflicts automatically,
presenting a review menu afterward.
The
.B patchpal
policy is an alternative for developers who prefer
.BR patchpal (1)
for cherry-pick conflict resolution.
The
.B edit
policy opens all conflicted files in the user's preferred editor
(configurable via
.BR \-\-editor ,
session, or
.BR $VISUAL / $EDITOR ;
defaults to
.BR vim ).
.PP
Clean cherry-picks proceed automatically while conflicts
in subsystem files invoke the chosen resolution strategy.
Out-of-scope conflicts (MAINTAINERS, files outside the target
subsystem) are still resolved automatically.
Build failures also present an interactive menu allowing the user
to fix failing files using their resolution strategy, skip the
commit, or abort.
.PP
The Conflicts stanza in commit messages is generated with
reviewer-style descriptions:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Dropped architecture hunks named explicitly
(e.g.\& \fIdropped loongarch, riscv hunks\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
API renames detected from the diff
(e.g.\& \fIrdmsrl->rdmsrq; in kvm_init()\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Missing prerequisites are detected
.B hunk\-first
before resolution: each conflict region’s
.B theirs
lines are mapped onto the upstream parent and blamed.
Commits are ranked by conflict lines owned (not whole\-file blame).
Caps:
50 blame lines per hunk,
30 distinct commits checked against RHEL history;
excess candidates are
.I unchecked
only — never listed as missing.
When missing commits are found,
.B isb
offers continue / skip / menu / quit
(not a hard exit).
A file is marked
.I likely blocked
when one missing commit owns most of its conflict lines.
Missing prerequisites are also grouped by series when they share keywords
(e.g.\& \fImissing rdmsrl, rename patch series (5 commit(s))\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Known upstream-only APIs flagged via
.BR api\-replacements.json .
.IP \(bu 2
Function names and change characterization
(e.g.\& \fIadded return value capture in sev_init()\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
When in-scope conflicts were resolved by automation, the stanza ends with
.BR Resolved\-by:\ isb
(Cursor AI Rule),
.BR Resolved\-by:\ patchpal ,
or
.BI Resolved\-by:\ <provider>
for another configured AI.
Manual editor resolutions omit this line.
.RE
.PP
After commit classification (Phase\ 3), the KEEP and OMIT lists are
written to editable files
.RI ( keep_commits.txt ", " omit_commits.txt )
in the output directory, and the script pauses with an explicit menu
.RB ( g
proceed,
.B v
review,
.B r
return,
.B q
quit).
.B isb
then seeks upstream commits that
.B Fixes:
a KEEP hash.
Out\-of\-scope dependents are added to OMIT (and later
.I Omitted\-fixes
/
.BR Omitted\-fix:
in the MR description) so tools like patchreview treat them as
intentionally omitted.
In\-scope dependents missing from Phase\ 2 are added to KEEP.
Before resolving conflicts,
.B isb
compares upstream functional
.B +/\-
lines to RHEL ours/HEAD (and any clean staged merge).
If nothing can land (no matching preimage, or additions already present),
it omits the commit without calling AI/edit.
After each cherry\-pick (and again after build),
.B isb
also omits commits whose upstream\-touched files retain no functional
staged changes while upstream had functional hunks
(already present on RHEL or unmergeable).
Unrelated build\-fix edits outside those files do not keep the commit.
Such omits are recorded in
.I Omitted\-fixes
with
.BR Omitted\-fix:
in the MR description.
The pre\-resolve missing\-prerequisite gate and post\-conflict AI
analysis both recommend SKIP when several prerequisites are missing
or hunks are kept\-as\-RHEL / dropped.
A missing\-prerequisite / kept\-RHEL heuristic always wins over a
mistaken AI ACCEPT.
Menus highlight
.B SKIP RECOMMENDED
and offer
.B s
to omit into
.IR Omitted\-fixes .
The
.B Conflicts:
stanza is written only when the cherry\-pick actually conflicted with
upstream.
Clean applies — including Kconfig commits that run mkmod validation,
or commits that only create a follow\-up
.B redhat/configs
snippet — do not get a Conflicts stanza.
When there was a conflict, non\-interactive isb AI ends the stanza with
an indented
.BR Resolved\-by:\ isb/cursor
or
.BI Resolved\-by:\ isb/cursor/ <model>
when an agent ran.
Interactive patchpal resolutions use
.B Assisted\-by:\ patchpal
instead (not Resolved\-by).
Manual in\-scope editor resolutions omit both.
.PP
Stable preferences (conflict/build policy, editor, ailog, AI provider,
API key path/env, model preference lists) are edited in the Config menu
.RB ( C )
and stored in
.I ~/.config/isb/config.json .
The API key secret is never written to that file; Config can paste a key
into
.I ~/.config/isb/api-keys/cursor
with mode 0600.
.PP
Phase\ 3 also inspects each commit's Kconfig diff for newly added
.B config FOO
options.
If
.I redhat/configs
has no matching
.B CONFIG_FOO
snippet, the keep entry is annotated
.RI ( needs redhat/configs for CONFIG_FOO )
and a
.B # NOTE:
comment is written into
.IR keep_commits.txt .
During cherry-pick, isb prompts to create
.I redhat/configs/common/generic/CONFIG_FOO
using the Kconfig default
.RB ( y / n / m )
or an explicit per-option choice.
After the upstream backport commit, isb commits the snippet(s) as a
separate
.B redhat/configs:
patch
.RB ( Upstream\ status:\ RHEL\-only )
for bisectability, using
.BR git\ commit\ \-s .
Any commit that touches Kconfig (or creates a snippet) forces an
mkmod-style build validation before those commits are finalized:
regenerate
.BR dist\-configs ,
install the host-arch
.I kernel\-*.config
as
.IR .config ,
run
.B make prepare
and
.BR make\ modules_prepare ,
then
.BI make\ M= subsystem .
This runs even when
.B \-\-build\-policy
is
.BR none .
.PP
Every long-running operation displays an animated spinner:
cherry-picking, conflict resolution, scope indexing, and build
testing.
The build spinner shows whether the build is post-conflict or
post-apply and which toolbox container is being used.
.PP
When the
.B patchpal
conflict policy is active and an in-scope conflict is found,
.B isb
displays a prominent notification banner directing the user to the
patchpal window, then attempts to raise it to the foreground using
.BR xdotool (1)
or
.BR wmctrl (1)
(if available).
.PP
If
.B isb
detects that HEAD has been reset externally (e.g.\& by
.BR "git reset" ),
it discards stale resume state and starts fresh, warning the user.
.PP
A
.B dist-configs
build is run inside a toolbox container to validate kernel configs,
with caching so subsequent runs skip the rebuild when HEAD is
unchanged.
.SH COMMANDS
When invoked without a command,
.B isb
runs the full backport flow.
.TP
.B status
Show all user\-config fields from
.I ~/.config/isb/config.json
(policies, editor, ailog, AI provider, API key location — never the
secret — model lists, Signed\-off\-by / Assisted\-by) and exit.
Does not require a kernel tree.
.TP
.B config
Open the interactive user Config menu (same as hotkey
.BR C
in Parameters), save on quit, and exit.
Does not require a kernel tree.
.TP
.B progress
Show backport progress (current HEAD, commits applied, next commit
in queue) and exit.
Must be run from a RHEL kernel tree with a saved session.
Does not require session parameters such as
.B \-\-subsystem
or
.BR \-\-rhel ;
reads them from the saved session file.
.TP
.BI "file " PATH
Backport the commits listed in
.I PATH
(same format as
.IR keep_commits.txt :
.BI hash
optionally followed by a subject; comments with
.B #
ignored).
Skips Phase\ 2 divergence discovery, Phase\ 3 KEEP/OMIT
classification, the Parameters menu, and the KEEP review menu;
the file is the KEEP list (oldest first) and cherry\-pick starts
immediately using the saved session for subsystem, scope, and
policies.
Does not rewrite
.I PATH
when it is already the session
.IR keep_commits.txt .
.TP
.BI "confirm " "[PATH]"
Review and edit the KEEP/OMIT commit list interactively, then exit.
With no
.IR PATH ,
uses the session
.IR keep_commits.txt .
If that default file is missing, prompts for a path.
If
.I PATH
is given but does not exist, prints a message and exits.
Updates
.IR keep_commits.txt ,
.IR omit_commits.txt ,
and the session cache; does not start cherry\-pick.
Also available as hotkey
.B v
in the Parameters menu.
.TP
.B continue
Resume from previously saved state in the output directory.
Offers the same proceed / review-commits menu as a fresh run so the
user can edit
.I keep_commits.txt
before cherry-picking continues.
.TP
.BI "pick " HASH
Cherry-pick a single upstream commit (by its full 40-character hash)
onto the current HEAD with conflict resolution and a RH Kernel Work
Flow specified commit message.
Skips the normal multi-commit workflow phases.
.TP
.BI "reset " HASH
Hard-reset the backport branch to an earlier commit and update saved
state so the next run resumes from the commit after
.IR HASH
(or from the first KEEP entry when
.I HASH
is the session base).
.I HASH
may be the pre\-backport session base (start over) or any commit in
.IR base..HEAD .
After the reset, offers an optional review of
.IR keep_commits.txt .
Also available interactively via the
.B r
key in the confirmation menu, which presents a numbered list of
applied commits.
Commits that had conflicts are marked with a leading red
.BR * .
.TP
.B new
Clear all persistent session data and exit.
Removes
.IR .backport/session.json ,
the dist-configs cache stamp, and any
.I .backport\-state.json
in the output directory.
The next invocation of
.B isb
will start a completely fresh session, prompting for all parameters.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-step
Single-step mode: apply only the next commit in the list, resolve
conflicts, build according to the build policy, then stop.
The user can inspect the result and resume with
.B isb \-\-step
to apply the next commit, or
.B isb continue
(or
.BR g )
to apply all remaining commits.
Also available interactively via the
.B x
key in the confirmation menu.
.TP
.B \-\-regen
Rewrite commit messages on the backport branch with the current session
values for JIRA, Upstream status, Signed-off-by, and Assisted-by, then
regenerate the patch series.
Useful after updating metadata fields (e.g.\ adding a real JIRA number)
without re-running the entire backport.
Also available interactively via the
.B p
key in the confirmation menu.
.TP
.B \-\-ailog\fR, \fB\-\-no\-ailog
Enable or disable AI-assisted conflict explanation and build-fix
(default: on).
When enabled,
.B isb
uses the Cursor SDK to call Claude in two situations:
.RS
.TP
.B Conflict explanation
After every conflict resolution, Claude reads the upstream diff,
the resolved diff, hunk\-first missing\-prerequisite rankings
(from conflict markers), and optional fallback blame data,
then produces a concise explanation appended to the Conflicts stanza in
the commit message and the conflict report file.
.TP
.B Iterative build-fix
When a build fails and the pattern-based auto-fix
.RB ( api\-replacements.json )
cannot resolve it,
.B isb
spawns a Cursor agent that examines the compiler errors, reads the
failing source files, and applies minimal fixes.
It then rebuilds and retries up to 3 times.
If all AI attempts fail,
.B isb
falls through to the manual Skip / Fix / Abort menu.
.RE
.IP
Requires the
.B cursor-sdk
Python package, the
.B CURSOR_API_KEY
environment variable, and a Cursor paid subscription.
If either the package or the key is missing,
.B isb
prints a one-time warning and falls back to local heuristic analysis:
.RS
.nf
CURSOR_API_KEY not set. Falling back to simpler conflict notes.
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B isb
automatically queries
.B Cursor.models.list()
at runtime and selects the best available model; no model name
needs to be configured.
.IP
To set up:
.RS
.nf
pip3 install cursor-sdk

# Generate a key at cursor.com/dashboard/api
# Click "New API Key" and copy the full secret (shown once)

# Save the key to a file:
echo "your-key-here" > ~/.cursor/cursor-worker-api-key

# Add to ~/.bashrc or ~/.bash_profile:
[ -f ~/.cursor/cursor-worker-api-key ] && \\
  export CURSOR_API_KEY="$(cat ~/.cursor/cursor-worker-api-key)"
.fi
.RE
.IP
Also available interactively via the
.B i
key in the confirmation menu.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
On startup,
.B isb
checks for required and optional tools:
.TP
.B Python 3.12+
Required.
If the running interpreter is too old, isb exits immediately.
.TP
.B toolbox
Optional; required for build testing if not building natively
(e.g.\& host OS differs from the RHEL target version).
If missing, isb offers to install it via
.BR "sudo dnf install toolbox" .
See
.B "TOOLBOX PATH RULES"
below for how repository locations are mapped into the container.
.TP
.B patchpal
Optional (GUI conflict resolution).
If missing, isb offers to enable the Copr repository and install it via
.BR "sudo dnf copr enable jbenc/patchpal" " and " "sudo dnf install patchpal" .
Requires a graphical display (X11 or Wayland); when no display is
detected, the patchpal option is shown in dim italic with a
.B **
marker in interactive menus.
The user may still select it, but isb will refuse to proceed at
runtime if no display is available.
.PP
When patchpal exits without saving or leaves conflicts unresolved,
.B isb
presents a mini-menu:
.B r
(retry patchpal),
.B s
(skip this commit), or
.B q
(quit isb).
Skipped commits are persisted and will be auto-skipped on subsequent
runs, including after a reset to zero.
They can be restored from the commit review page.
Interactive skips prompt for an omit reason; automatic isb/AI omits
(inapplicable, already applied, etc.) record their reason.
Both rewrite
.I omit_commits.txt
immediately (and trim
.IR keep_commits.txt ).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Session parameters (prompted interactively or via CLI)"
These are saved to
.I .backport/session.json
and reused across runs.
They can also be provided on the command line to override session values.
.TP
.BI \-\-subsystem " PATH"
Subsystem directory relative to the kernel tree root,
e.g.\&
.IR drivers/char/ipmi .
Used for scope-path auto-discovery and branch naming.
.TP
.BI \-\-rhel " VERSION"
RHEL version (e.g.\&
.BR 9 ", " 10 ", " 10.3 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-upstream\-repo " DIR"
Path to the upstream Linux kernel git repository.
.TP
.BI \-\-upstream\-version " TAG"
Upstream version tag that marks the end of the range,
e.g.\&
.BR v7.1 " or " v7.1\-rc3 .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signed\-off\-by " " \fI"NAME <EMAIL>"\fR
Signed-off-by trailer added to every commit message.
If not provided on the command line and not saved in the session,
.B isb
auto-detects it from
.BR "git config user.name" " and " "git config user.email" .
If auto-detection fails, the user is prompted interactively.
Also editable as field
.B 9
in the confirmation menu.
.SS "Optional arguments"
.TP
.BI \-\-jira " URL"
JIRA ticket URL.
Default:
.BR RHEL\-PLACEHOLDER .
.TP
.BI \-\-extra\-paths " PATH ..."
Additional scope paths to include alongside the auto-discovered ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " PATH ..."
Use only these scope paths and skip auto-discovery entirely.
Useful for catch-up runs on specific directories such as
Documentation or include headers.
.TP
.BI \-\-branch " NAME"
Branch name for the backport.
If the branch does not exist, it is created from the current HEAD
at the start of Phase 4 (cherry-picking).
If it already exists, it is checked out.
Default: the leaf component of the
.B \-\-subsystem
path (e.g.\&
.I drivers/char/ipmi
produces branch
.IR ipmi ).
.PP
When running interactively with no saved branch (or when a previously
saved branch no longer exists),
.B isb
presents a branch selection menu:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If the repo has a single branch, the user may press
.B n
to create a new branch or press
.B Enter
to work on the current branch.
.IP \(bu 2
If 2\(en9 branches exist, they are listed with single\-keypress
selection (1\(en9), plus
.B n
to create a new branch or
.B Enter
to stay on the current branch.
.IP \(bu 2
If 10 or more branches exist, the user is prompted to type a
branch name, enter
.B n
to create a new branch, or press
.B Enter
to stay on the current branch.
.RE
.PP
Pressing
.B Enter
without input warns that all work will be performed on
the current branch.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Output directory for patches, reports, and saved state.
Default:
.IR <rhel\-repo>/../backports/<rhel>/<subsystem>/ .
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict\-policy " POLICY"
How to handle in-scope file conflicts.
Default:
.B ai
(Cursor AI Rule)
if cursor\-sdk and CURSOR_API_KEY are available, then
.B patchpal
if a graphical display and patchpal are present, otherwise
.BR edit .
.RS
.TP
.B patchpal
Launch the
.BR patchpal (1)
GUI for interactive three-way merge resolution.
Requires an active graphical display
.RB ( DISPLAY
or
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY )
and
.B patchpal
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B edit
Open conflicted files in the user's editor for manual resolution.
See
.BR \-\-editor .
.TP
.B ai " (Cursor AI Rule)"
Use a Cursor Opus agent to resolve conflicts automatically.
Requires
.B cursor\-sdk
and
.BR CURSOR_API_KEY .
The agent receives the conflict markers, upstream context, and RHEL
specifics, then edits each file to remove all conflict markers.
After AI resolution, the standard review menu lets you accept the
result, re-do (falls back to the editor), or skip the commit.
The AI follows the methodology in
.I .cursor/rules/conflict\-analysis.mdc
\(em preserve RHEL-specific code, apply upstream changes, and verify
lock/unlock correctness on all code paths.
If the SDK or API key is unavailable, isb falls back to
.BR edit .
.TP
.B abort
Abort the cherry-pick on any in-scope conflict.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-editor " CMD"
Editor command for the
.B edit
conflict policy and build-failure fixes.
Saved in the session file.
Default: vim (fallback: vi); also respects
.B $VISUAL
and
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.BI \-\-build\-policy " POLICY"
When to run a build test inside the toolbox container.
Incremental builds install the host\-arch
.B .config
and run
.B modules_prepare
when needed, then
.BI "make M=" subsystem .
Failures are fail\-closed:
.B fatal error
diagnostics and other non\-modpost make failures count as build
failures (an unprepared tree can no longer silently pass).
Kconfig\-touching commits use a fuller mkmod\-style validation.
.RS
.TP
.B conflict
Build only after commits that had conflicts (default).
.TP
.B every
Build after every commit.
.TP
.B none
Never build.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Classify and list upstream commits without cherry-picking.
Useful for reviewing what would be applied before committing to a run.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the interactive confirmation menu and proceed immediately.
.TP
.BI \-\-assisted\-by " \"NAME <EMAIL>\""
Assisted-by trailer added to commit messages, e.g.\&
.IR "Claude <no-reply@anthropic.com>" .
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " HASH ..."
Exclude specific commit(s) by hash prefix.
Multiple hashes may be given.
User-skipped commits are persisted in the state file and will be
auto-skipped on subsequent runs, even after a reset to zero.
They can be restored from the interactive commit review page.
.TP
.BI \-\-divergence " HASH"
Upstream commit hash to use as the divergence point, skipping
automatic detection.
.TP
.BI \-\-toolbox " NAME"
Toolbox container name for
.B dist-configs
builds.
Default:
.BI rhel RHEL
(e.g.\&
.BR rhel10 ).
See
.B "TOOLBOX PATH RULES"
for supported repository locations.
.SH MODES
.SS "Full backport (default)"
Discovers scope paths from the subsystem directory, finds the
divergence point between upstream and RHEL, lists pending
upstream commits, and cherry-picks them in order.
Each commit receives a RH Kernel Work Flow specified message with JIRA, upstream status,
conflict notes, the original commit metadata, and sign-off trailers.
.SS "Single-commit pick (pick)"
Cherry-picks exactly one upstream commit onto the current HEAD.
The upstream repository is automatically configured as a git remote
if needed, ensuring the commit object is reachable.
Requires
.B \-\-subsystem
for scope-path context.
.SH SESSIONS
.B isb
automatically manages a session file at
.I .backport/session.json
in the current repository.
There are no flags to manage sessions; they are handled transparently.
.SS "Interactive mode (no CLI arguments)"
When invoked without session-level arguments on the command line,
an interactive menu is displayed showing all parameters with their
current values (loaded from the saved session, if any).
.PP
Each parameter is labelled with a single\-character hotkey
.RB ( s ", " r ", " u ", " v ", " b ", " j ", " t ", " p ", " c ", " d ", " a ", " o ", " e ).
Press a key to edit that parameter.
All menu input is single\-keypress (no Enter required).
.PP
Press
.B g
to accept all values and proceed (Go).
If required fields (marked with
.BR * )
are not set,
.B g
will refuse and list what is missing.
Options that require a graphical display (such as
.BR patchpal )
are marked with
.B **
and shown in dim italic when no display is detected.
Press
.B q
to abort.
.PP
.B Scope paths
are listed with uppercase letter keys
.RB ( A ", " B ", " C ", ...);
press a letter to edit that path (leave empty to remove it).
Press
.B +
to add a new scope path.
.PP
.B Additional hotkeys
in the confirmation menu:
.TP
.B g
Go: proceed with apply\-all using cached classification when available.
.TP
.B G
Clear cached divergence/KEEP/OMIT results and apply all
(forces phases 1\-3 to re\-run).
Shown only when cached results are present.
Does not reset cherry\-pick progress.
.TP
.B r
Reset to an earlier backport commit; a numbered list of applied
commits is shown for selection; commits that had conflicts are marked
with a leading red
.BR * .
.TP
.B p
Regenerate patches with current session field values
(same as
.BR \-\-regen ).
.TP
.B x
Single-step mode: apply only the next commit and stop
(same as
.BR \-\-step ).
.TP
.B h
Display the man page.
.TP
.B v
Commit review: display all KEEP and OMIT commits.
Previously omitted commits appear with a red
.B \(mu
mark and their omit reason.
Toggle any entry by number to move it between keep and omit.
User-skipped commits restored here are also removed from the
persisted skip list.
.TP
.B N
New session: walk through all parameters sequentially for editing.
Also clears completion state and progress, allowing a full restart.
.PP
When editing a field, type a new value and press Enter.
Press Enter on an empty line to keep the current value,
or type
.B clear
to remove an optional field.
The
.B toolbox
field presents a numbered list of available toolbox containers
(from
.BR "toolbox list" )
for single\-keypress selection.
.SS "Command-line mode"
When session-level arguments are provided on the command line,
they are merged into the saved session silently, with no
interactive menu.
This allows scripted and non\-interactive usage.
.SS "Sticky parameters"
All session parameters carry their values across invocations
as defaults in the interactive menu.
.SS "Transient flags"
.BR \-\-dry\-run ", " \-\-yes ", and "
.B \-\-divergence
are per-invocation and never saved to the session.
The
.BR continue ", " pick ", " reset ", " status ", " config ", " progress ,
.BR confirm ", " file
commands are also per-invocation.
.SH EXAMPLES
First run (prompts for session parameters):
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd ~/kernels/centos\-9
isb \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Subsequent runs reuse the session automatically:
.PP
.RS
.nf
isb
isb \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pick a single upstream commit (session provides the rest):
.PP
.RS
.nf
